<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  chocolatey/filehasher.nuspec: package metadata for the Chocolatey
  Community Repository (https://community.chocolatey.org/packages/filehasher).

  This file is a TEMPLATE. Two values are version-specific and are
  substituted by the `chocolatey-push` CI job before `choco pack` runs
  (see chocolatey/README.md for the local-render snippet):

    {VERSION}        the release version, e.g. 0.4.0 (no leading "v")
    {RELEASE_NOTES}  prose summary + release URL, built from
                     winget/release-notes.txt (shared with the winget job)

  Everything else is static and should stay in sync with the csproj metadata,
  installer/FileHasher.wxs, and winget/manifest-templates/.

  Chocolatey naming conventions applied here (docs.chocolatey.org, "Naming
  Your Package"): the id is the CamelCase app name lowercased with no dots
  ("filehasher"), and the title carries the official spelling ("FileHasher").
  No .install/.portable suffix: only one form of the package is published, so
  the plain id is correct.

  packageSourceUrl, projectSourceUrl, docsUrl and bugTrackerUrl are Chocolatey
  extensions that NuGet's own nuspec schema does not define, so this file must
  be packed with `choco pack`; `nuget pack` errors on them.

  The iconUrl is pinned to the icon file's own commit SHA on the GitHub
  mirror, served through jsDelivr. GitHub raw links are a hard rejection
  (CPMR0076) and branch URLs defeat the CDN's permanent cache, so the SHA
  form is the one to keep. Only change it if the icon artwork changes.
-->
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2015/06/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>filehasher</id>
    <version>{VERSION}</version>
    <packageSourceUrl>https://github.com/fsantiago07044/filehasher/tree/main/chocolatey</packageSourceUrl>
    <!--
      authors = the software vendor; owners = the package maintainer. They are
      deliberately the same organization here, which the validator flags as a
      Note (CPMR0068) for the reviewer's benefit; no action is needed, the
      vendor maintains this package.
    -->
    <owners>FSP Productions, LLC</owners>
    <title>FileHasher</title>
    <authors>FSP Productions, LLC</authors>
    <projectUrl>https://www.fspproductions.com/software-projects</projectUrl>
    <iconUrl>https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/fsantiago07044/filehasher@41f3e822e071029f5f986b02465de3c99690a28e/FileHasherApp/assets/app-icon/hash-icon-128.png</iconUrl>
    <copyright>Copyright © 2026 FSP Productions, LLC</copyright>
    <licenseUrl>https://github.com/fsantiago07044/filehasher/blob/main/LICENSE</licenseUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <projectSourceUrl>https://github.com/fsantiago07044/filehasher</projectSourceUrl>
    <docsUrl>https://github.com/fsantiago07044/filehasher/blob/main/README.md</docsUrl>
    <bugTrackerUrl>https://github.com/fsantiago07044/filehasher/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
    <tags>filehasher hash hashing checksum sha256 sha512 md5 sha1 integrity verification sidecar csv gui foss admin</tags>
    <summary>Windows GUI utility to hash files and folders, write sidecar hash files, verify them, and export results to CSV.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[
FileHasher is a Windows desktop utility for computing and checking
cryptographic file hashes. It hashes a single file or an entire folder tree,
writes and verifies `sha256sum`-style sidecar files, and exports full results
to CSV.

**Features**

* MD5, SHA1, SHA256, and SHA512, selected per run.
* Recursive folder scans with an extension filter (defaults to `.exe` and
  `.msi`; one checkbox widens it to every file type).
* Sidecar hash files in three formats: `sha256sum` style, hash only, and an
  extended format that also records the file's last-modified timestamp (ISO
  8601 UTC) and size in bytes. The suggested extension follows the selected
  algorithm.
* **Verify Sidecars** re-hashes a tree and compares it against the sidecars
  already on disk, auto-detecting each sidecar's algorithm from its hash
  length, so a mixed-algorithm tree verifies in a single pass. Per-file
  verdicts cover matches, mismatches, missing files, and files that have no
  sidecar at all.
* CSV export of every result, including file size and last-modified time.
* Right-click a result to open its folder in File Explorer, open a PowerShell
  or Command Prompt there, or copy the hash or the file path.
* Experimental option to open `.msi` packages read-only and hash the files
  contained inside them individually, in addition to the `.msi` itself.
* Per-run logs, and in-app help covering every feature.

**Licensing**

Free and open source under the MIT license. Not trial software; there is
nothing to activate and no license key to enter.

**Package notes**

* This package downloads the Authenticode-signed MSI from the project's
  official GitHub release and verifies its SHA256 checksum before installing.
* Per-machine install (administrator rights required) to
  `%ProgramFiles%\FileHasher`, with a Start Menu shortcut and an Add/Remove
  Programs entry. Upgrades replace the previous version in place, leaving a
  single entry.
* The executable is self-contained: no .NET runtime needs to be installed.
* 64-bit Windows only. Windows 10 1809 (build 17763) or newer.
]]></description>
    <releaseNotes>{RELEASE_NOTES}</releaseNotes>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
  </files>
</package>
